     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Two controls are enough: mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin. Phishing is always available, so the attacker always gets foothold_workstation and stolen_user. That foothold also gets around mfa_user. With foothold_workstation the attacker can reach the internal network and get stolen_admin (via appserver or ciserver and kerberoasting) and stolen_dbadmin (via leak_secrets_ciserver). Every exfiltration path needs admin_db, admin_fileserver or admin_backup. Each of those needs an auth_* technique that requires either NOT mfa_dbadmin or admin_dc/admin_adfs. mfa:dbadmin blocks the first option. mfa:admin blocks auth_dc and auth_adfs, which are the only ways to get a foothold on the dc or adfs, so admin_dc and admin_adfs can never be reached. No single control is enough. With mfa:dbadmin alone, the attacker can still go auth_dc, then escalate_dc, then admin_dc, then auth_db. With mfa:admin alone, stolen_dbadmin from the ciserver still allows auth_db. Segmented alone is bypassed by escalate_workstation, which gives admin_workstation and allows the pivot. No patch blocks the internal auth or escalate steps that lead to exfiltration. The minimum is therefore 2.\"}"
    }
